    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Broadly, this research considers South Bay University’s credit evaluation process. Specifically, this research is designed to answer the question, How does an elite highly-selective 4-year institution’s transfer credit evaluation processes affect a transfer student’s degree progress? One theory or concept integral to this research is the concept of discrepancies that exist within departmental structures that give a double class standing to a transfer student based on the number of units they transfer in. Previous research influencing this study was conducted by Barbara Townsend, Stephen J Handel, and Alexander Paul Ott in 2006, 2007, and 2015, respectively, to show that a positive transfer student experience is contingent upon a supportive receiving institution that limits the amount of barriers they encounter upon enrollment. In the past South Bay University hasn’t had articulation agreements or partnerships that have impacted the evaluation process. This mixed methods study works with a data set of survey results and interviews of current South Bay transfer students. This data is then analyzed by using qualitative data as a supplemental consideration to the quantitative data that participants offer. A key challenge posed by this research is understanding the nuanced transfer credit evaluation process, that this study addressed by examining transfer student experience and administrative processes. Main findings of this study are that there is a lack of support prior to enrollment and after their first enrollment within respective academic departments, and that students are faced with having to make quick decisions that impact their academic journeys based on their desired time to attend South Bay. This study aims to contribute to the student experience with South Bay’s transfer credit evaluation process and seek institution change with participant recommendations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
